## 9. Histogram with ggplot2

Start a figure with `ggplot()`, map `body_mass_g` to the x-axis, and add `geom_histogram()`. Use `penguins_joined` (or `penguins_clean`). Add a title with `labs(title = "...")`.



::: {.cell envir='wrangle_viz' setup='true' exercise='ggplot_histogram'}
```{webr}
#| envir: wrangle_viz
#| setup: true
#| exercise: ggplot_histogram
library(tidyverse)
library(palmerpenguins)
penguins_clean <- penguins |>
  filter(!is.na(body_mass_g))
island_tidy <- data.frame(
  Biscoe = 120,
  Dream = 85,
  Torgersen = 45
) |>
  pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = "island", values_to = "camp_elevation_m")
penguins_joined <- penguins_clean |>
  left_join(island_tidy, by = "island")
```
:::

::: {.cell envir='wrangle_viz' exercise='ggplot_histogram'}
